1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Section 3

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,105
Jack:

3
00:00:02,105 --> 00:00:06,800
I've still got loads to do for our report on nutritional food labels.

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,008
Alice:

5
00:00:08,008 --> 00:00:08,169
Me too.

6
00:00:08,169 --> 00:00:13,200
What did you learn from doing the project about your own shopping habits?

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:13,250
Jack:

8
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:19,044
Well, I've always had to check labels for traces of peanuts in everything I eat

9
00:00:19,044 --> 00:00:20,506
because of my allergy.

10
00:00:20,506 --> 00:00:25,619
But beyond that I've never really been concerned enough to check how healthy a

11
00:00:25,619 --> 00:00:26,350
product is.

12
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:26,780
Alice:

13
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:32,370
This project has actually taught me to read the labels much more carefully.

14
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:37,100
I tended to believe claims on packaging like 'low in fat'.

15
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:44,000
But I now realise that the 'healthy' yoghurt I've bought for years is full of sugar

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,450
and that it's actually quite high in calories.

17
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:47,886
Jack:

18
00:00:47,886 --> 00:00:50,067
Ready meals are the worst ...

19
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:54,429
comparing the labels on supermarket pizzas was a real eye-opener.

20
00:00:54,429 --> 00:00:58,791
Did you have any idea how many calories they contain?

21
00:00:58,791 --> 00:01:00,100
I was amazed.

22
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:00,546
Alice:

23
00:01:00,546 --> 00:01:00,991
Yes,

24
00:01:00,991 --> 00:01:07,230
because unless you read the label really carefully, you wouldn't know that the nutritional

25
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:10,350
values given are for half a pizza.

26
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:10,754
Jack:

27
00:01:10,754 --> 00:01:13,581
When most people eat the whole pizza.

28
00:01:13,581 --> 00:01:15,600
Not exactly transparent is it?

29
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:16,057
Alice:

30
00:01:16,057 --> 00:01:17,429
Not at all.

31
00:01:17,429 --> 00:01:22,000
But I expect it won't stop you from buying pizza?

32
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:22,455
Jack:

33
00:01:22,455 --> 00:01:23,821
Probably not, no!

34
00:01:23,821 --> 00:01:29,738
I thought comparing the different labelling systems used by food manufacturers was interesting.

35
00:01:29,738 --> 00:01:35,200
I think the kind of labelling system used makes a big difference.

36
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:35,592
Alice:

37
00:01:35,592 --> 00:01:37,550
Which one did you prefer?

38
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:38,015
Jack:

39
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:44,057
I liked the traditional daily value system best - the one which tells you

40
00:01:44,057 --> 00:01:50,100
what proportion of your required daily intake of each ingredient the product contains.

41
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,403
I'm not sure it's the easiest for people to use

42
00:01:53,403 --> 00:01:56,045
but at least you get the full story.

43
00:01:56,045 --> 00:02:01,329
I like to know all the ingredients in a product - not just how much fat, salt

44
00:02:01,329 --> 00:02:02,650
and sugar they contain.

45
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:03,119
Alice:

46
00:02:03,119 --> 00:02:09,681
But it's good supermarkets have been making an effort to provide reliable information for

47
00:02:09,681 --> 00:02:10,150
customers.

48
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:10,600
Jack:

49
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:11,050
Yes.

50
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:17,350
There just needs to be more consistency between labelling systems used by different supermarkets,

51
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:20,050
in terms of portion sizes, etc.

52
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:20,500
Alice:

53
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:20,950
Mmm.

54
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:27,250
The labels on the different brands of chicken flavour crisps were quite revealing too,

55
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:28,150
weren't they?

56
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:28,610
Jack:

57
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:29,069
Yeah.

58
00:02:29,069 --> 00:02:35,502
I don't understand how they can get away with calling them chicken flavour when

59
00:02:35,502 --> 00:02:37,800
they only contain artificial additives.

60
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:38,229
Alice:

61
00:02:38,229 --> 00:02:39,088
I know.

62
00:02:39,088 --> 00:02:45,100
I'd at least have expected them to contain a small percentage of real chicken.

63
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:45,975
Jack:

64
00:02:45,975 --> 00:02:46,850
Absolutely.

65
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:47,265
Alice:

66
00:02:47,265 --> 00:02:52,660
I think having nutritional food labeling has been a good idea, don't you?

67
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:58,470
I think it will change people's behaviour and stop mothers, in particular, buying the

68
00:02:58,470 --> 00:02:59,300
wrong things.

69
00:02:59,300 --> 00:02:59,779
Jack:

70
00:02:59,779 --> 00:03:04,094
But didn't that study kind of prove the opposite?

71
00:03:04,094 --> 00:03:07,450
People didn't necessarily stop buying unhealthy products.

72
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:07,860
Alice:

73
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,143
They only said that might be the case.

74
00:03:11,143 --> 00:03:16,067
Those findings weren't that conclusive and it was quite a small-scale study.

75
00:03:16,067 --> 00:03:19,350
I think more research has to be done.

76
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:19,729
Jack:

77
00:03:19,729 --> 00:03:22,000
Yes, I think you're probably right.

78
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:22,389
Jack:

79
00:03:22,389 --> 00:03:25,500
What do you think of the traffic-light system?

80
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:25,917
Alice:

81
00:03:25,917 --> 00:03:31,342
I think supermarkets like the idea of having a colour-coded system - red, orange

82
00:03:31,342 --> 00:03:36,350
or green - for levels of fat, sugar and salt in a product.

83
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:36,731
Jack:

84
00:03:36,731 --> 00:03:39,014
But it's not been adopted universally.

85
00:03:39,014 --> 00:03:40,917
And not on all products.

86
00:03:40,917 --> 00:03:43,200
Why do you suppose that is?

87
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:43,614
Alice:

88
00:03:43,614 --> 00:03:45,682
Pressure from the food manufacturers.

89
00:03:45,682 --> 00:03:51,473
Hardly surprising that some of them are opposed to flagging up how unhealthy their

90
00:03:51,473 --> 00:03:52,300
products are.

91
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:52,646
Jack:

92
00:03:52,646 --> 00:03:55,418
I'd have thought it would have been compulsory.

93
00:03:55,418 --> 00:03:57,150
It seems ridiculous it isn't.

94
00:03:57,150 --> 00:03:57,533
Alice:

95
00:03:57,533 --> 00:03:58,298
I know.

96
00:03:58,298 --> 00:04:03,657
And what I couldn't get over is the fact that it was brought in

97
00:04:03,657 --> 00:04:08,250
without enough consultation - a lot of experts had deep reservations about it.

98
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:08,622
Jack:

99
00:04:08,622 --> 00:04:10,480
That is a bit weird.

100
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,685
I suppose there's an argument for doing the research now when consumers are familiar

101
00:04:15,685 --> 00:04:16,800
with this system.

102
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,483
Alice:

103
00:04:17,483 --> 00:04:18,850
Yeah, maybe.

104
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:19,246
Jack:

105
00:04:19,246 --> 00:04:24,000
The participants in the survey were quite positive about the traffic-light system.

106
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:24,405
Alice:

107
00:04:24,405 --> 00:04:24,810
Mmm.

108
00:04:24,810 --> 00:04:28,453
But I don't think they targeted the right people.

109
00:04:28,453 --> 00:04:32,502
They should have focused on people with low literacy levels

110
00:04:32,502 --> 00:04:36,550
because these labels are designed to be accessible to them.

111
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:36,985
Jack:

112
00:04:36,985 --> 00:04:37,420
Yeah.

113
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:41,770
But it's good to get feedback from all socio-economic groups.

114
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:45,250
And there wasn't much variation in their responses.

115
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:45,688
Alice:

116
00:04:45,688 --> 00:04:46,126
No.

117
00:04:46,126 --> 00:04:52,258
But if they hadn't interviewed participants face-to-face, they could have used a much bigger

118
00:04:52,258 --> 00:04:53,134
sample size.

119
00:04:53,134 --> 00:04:56,200
I wonder why they chose that method?

120
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:56,685
Jack:

121
00:04:56,685 --> 00:04:57,169
Dunno.

122
00:04:57,169 --> 00:04:59,108
How were they selected?

123
00:04:59,108 --> 00:05:02,500
Did they volunteer or were they approached?

124
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:02,915
Alice:

125
00:05:02,915 --> 00:05:04,574
I think they volunteered.

126
00:05:04,574 --> 00:05:09,967
The thing that wasn't stated was how often they bought packaged food - all

127
00:05:09,967 --> 00:05:13,700
we know is how frequently they used the supermarket.
